A||N||4||kinboyatuwo||Help with suspension PLEASE!||||1||
Z||000000||kinboyatuwo||09-25-2000||03:34 PM||||Ok i have a 87 4 cylinder needing a little tlc.  The front upper control arm has a lot, i really mean a lot, of play.  I checked the manual and they say to replace the whole thing.  The fiero shop has bushings listed, do they work, should i get them?<BR>if so how the heck do i install them?<BR>If you have them tell me if they are good<BR>any help is great.  If i get them should i just get the whole kit and replace all of them?<P>thanks<BR>from a poor university student<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||09-25-2000||05:20 PM||||What poor university are you a student at   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?<P>Sounds like a fair amount of work, based upon what I have read here: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005387.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005387.html</A> <P>My suggestions:<P>First make <B>sure</B> it's the bushings causing the undesirable play in the UCA.  Next:  <P>If you can't live without your car for awhile, I would suggest pulling the UCA + pivot shaft from a salvaged vehicle.  That way, you get some no-cost experience getting the things off.<P>Take it home, rebush, then some weekend replace the existing UCA+pivot shaft. <P>Warning: I've never done the above - it's pretty much the "voice of inexperience" talking!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Last time I had to rebush a control arm (non-Fiero), I took it to a service station.  Gave the control arm to a mechanic who looked bored - he R&R'ed the bushings for $5 cash. <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-25-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000002||Bob Englert||09-25-2000||06:51 PM||||Replacing the upper control arm bushing is pretty easy on the 87.  The Haynes manual has pretty good instructions and I would strongly recopmmend you pick one up at your local auto parts store for the step by step.<P>You will need a floor jack to support the lower control arm when you separate the upper control arm from the spindle.  Rodney Dickman sells a good ball joint separator tool that does not damage the upper ball joint like a pickle fork can.  <P>Basic instructions include:<P>1) Remove tire!<BR>2) Support lower control arm with floor jack.<BR>3) Remove cotter pin from upper ball joint stud.  Loosen but do not remove upper ball joint nut that secures the ball joint to the spindle.<BR>4) Break the spindle loose from the upper ball joint stud.  It is a tapered fit and will be to be pressed out with Rodney's tool or a similar separating tool.  A pickle fork can also be used but often damages the grease boot.  If you will be replacing the upper ball joint while it is out, then hammer away!<BR>5) With the ball joint separated from the spindle, remove the nut and swing the upper control arm up to clear the ball joint.  Be sure the floor jack is supporting the force of the spring and lower control arm.<BR>6) Soak the upper control arm bolt in penetrating oil.  Note the position of the spacer washers between the frame and control arm bushings.  There should be one on either side of the frameand both should add up to 12mm thick.<BR>7) Remove the upper control arm nut and pull the bolt forward to remove.  If the bolt is rusted to the inner sleeve of the bushing, you might need to heat the bushing or bolt with a torch and bang on it to break it free.<BR>8) Take the upper control arm to a auto machine shop and have them press out the old and press in the new bushings.  I Would suggest that you replace the upper ball joint while it is out.<BR>9) reassemble in reverse order, torque averthing to specs, and get the car aligned.<P>Your car should feel much better and quieter if things were as sloppy as they were on my daughter's 87 coupe.<P>Good Luck!!!||||reg||12
Z||000003||Bob Englert||09-25-2000||06:54 PM||||Forgot to mention, you DO NOT have to replace the entire control arm.  Most auto parts stores can sell you just the bushings and upper ball joint.  I got mine at AutoZone.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000004||kinboyatuwo||09-26-2000||12:45 PM||||You guys are the greatist, thanks, will do it in the next couple weeks<P>by the way the haynes manual sayes you have to replace the whole control arm and there are no instructions to replace the bushings.<P>I am a proud student of the University of Western Ontario<BR>the best in Canada<P>Thanks to all who helped!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||14
